       The ssttrrsseepp() function was introduced as a replacement for ssttrrttookk(3),
       since the latter cannot handle empty fields.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       Be cautious when using this function.  If you do use it, note that:

       •  This function modifies its first argument.

       •  This function cannot be used on constant strings.

       •  The identity of the delimiting character is lost.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The program below is a port of  the  one  found  in  ssttrrttookk(3),  which,
       however, doesn’t discard multiple delimiters or empty tokens:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt ''aa//bbbbbb//////cccc;;xxxxxx::yyyyyy::'' ''::;;'' ''//''
           1: a/bbb///cc
                    --> a
                    --> bbb
                    -->
                    -->
                    --> cc
           2: xxx
                    --> xxx
           3: yyy
                    --> yyy
           4:
                    -->

   PPrrooggrraamm ssoouurrccee

       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <string.h>

       int
       main(int argc, char *argv[])
       {
           char *token, *subtoken;

           if (argc != 4) {
               fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s string delim subdelim\n", argv[0]);
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           for (unsigned int j = 1; (token = strsep(&argv[1], argv[2])); j++) {
               printf("%u: %s\n", j, token);

               while ((subtoken = strsep(&token, argv[3])))
                   printf("\t --> %s\n", subtoken);
           }

           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

